 the studio's pre-release projection that was exceeded. Two additional defects are bounded: the complete absence of ## section headings despite the commission spec requiring three to five, and a near-duplicate sentence pattern in paragraph 4 flagged by deterministic copy-edit standards. All three defects are repairable without replay.","requiredRepairs":["Correct the domestic opening figure in paragraph 2: the manuscript states 'an opening of approximately $117 million,' but witnessed sources (LA Times, WinterIsComing) establish $124.5 million as the actual result; $117 million was the studio's internal estimate, which the actual result exceeded.","Insert four ## section headings to satisfy the commission form spec's three-to-five section structural requirement; the draft has zero headings, and the deterministic standards specifically flagged demand-lens-missing-h2 as a warning.","Resolve the paragraph-4 near-duplicate sentence structure ('The production budget was reported between $200 million and $250 million, placing it among... The picture is a big-budget adaptation of Homer's ancient epic poem') by merging the two consecutive 'The [noun]...' constructions into one."],"report":"**Pipeline Diagnosis**\n\nThe failure originates in the writer-model layer. The assignment is well-formed, the sources are adequate for the brief, and the evidence budget supports the reported content. The sourcing packet from LAT, Variety, WinterIsComing, Deadline, and Times of India converges on all core figures. The failure is a precision error in the writer's handling of the LAT data, compounded by a structural omission and a copy-editing lapse.\n\n**Material Factual Error â€” Domestic Opening Figure (Paragraph 2)**\n\nThe manuscript states: \"Domestically, Universal Pictures reported an opening of approximately $117 million, which the Los Angeles Times described as a record-setting live-action debut for the studio and well above internal estimates.\"\n\nThis sentence is factually inverted. The LA Times source (claim bd7c6f:2) reads: the film \"outperformed studio expectations of a $117-million domestic opening\" â€” meaning $117 million was the studio's pre-release projection, and the actual reported domestic haul was $124.5 million. The WinterIsComing source (claim fb291f6c:10) independently corroborates the $124.5 million figure and further notes that The Dark Knight Rises' $160 million domestic figure still exceeds The Odyssey's $124 million domestic result. The manuscript's sentence does the opposite of what the source says: it reports the estimate as the outcome, then describes the actual result as \"well above internal estimates\" â€” a contradiction embedded within the same sentence. This is a material factual error, not a paraphrase issue. The corrected sentence must identify $124.5 million as the actual domestic opening and $117 million as the exceeded expectation.\n\nFixing this sentence simultaneously resolves the deterministic near-duplicate-proximity warning in paragraph 2, which flagged the consecutive uses of \"approximately\" in \"approximately $249 million\" and \"approximately $117 million\" â€” the corrected domestic sentence removes the second instance.\n\n**Structural Omission â€” Missing Section Headings**\n\nThe commission specification requires three to five ## sections. The draft has zero. The deterministic standards logged this as a demand-lens-missing-h2 warning rather than a blocking gate failure, but a complete absence is a more severe departure than the warning language implies. The continuous prose does have natural section breaks: (1) lede and record context; (2) international territory performance; (3) film profile, budget, and casting; (4) IMAX and production methods; (5) reception context and the theatrical outlook. Four headings can be inserted before paragraphs 3, 4, 6, and 9 respectively to create a conforming four-section structure with a lede, covering the demand lens naturally in at least one heading.\n\n**Near-Duplicate Sentence Structure â€” Paragraph 4**\n\nThe deterministic standards flagged a second near-duplicate-proximity issue in paragraph 4: \"The production budget was reported between $200 million and $250 million, placing it among / The picture is a big-budget adaptation of Homer's ancient epic poem, per Times of India, p.\" Two consecutive sentences both open with \"The [noun]...\" and carry parallel attributive constructions. The repair merges the budget range and the film's source material into a single sentence by appending the adaptation clause after an em-dash, eliminating the repetitive opening pattern without dropping any sourced fact.\n\n**Headline, Lede, and Summary Audit**\n\nThe headline (\"Nolan's Odyssey Reaches New Heights with $264.1M Global Debut\") is accurate and supported: the $264.1M global figure is corroborated by all five sources, and the record claim is correct at the global level. The lede correctly attributes the claim to the LA Times and Variety and preserves appropriate modality. The summary does not repeat the $117M error â€” it references only the $264.1M global figure â€” and is factually clean. After body repairs, repair_editorial_surfaces should regenerate the summary and description to ensure consistency with the corrected body.\n\n**Other Claims Verified Against Witnessed Evidence**\n\nâ€” $249M worldwide for Dark Knight Rises: confirmed by WinterIsComing (claim fb291f6c:10) and Deadline (claim deadline:1). âœ“  \nâ€” $15M gap between records: WinterIsComing states \"over $15 million.\" âœ“  \nâ€” 45 international markets as Nolan's biggest ever: WinterIsComing. âœ“  \nâ€” France, Italy, Spain, Poland, Netherlands, Brazil records: WinterIsComing citing Deadline (claim fb291f6c:14). âœ“  \nâ€” International total ahead of Oppenheimer: Deadline (claim deadline:2). âœ“  \nâ€” Variety's R-rating, length, no franchise affiliation characterization: supported (claim variety:2). âœ“  \nâ€” $200Mâ€“$250M production budget: LAT (claim bd7c6f:5) and TOI (claim toi:4). âœ“  \nâ€” Matt Damon as Odysseus, Zendaya as Athena: WinterIsComing (claim fb291f6c:8). âœ“  \nâ€” Casting controversy, conservative commentary: TOI (claim toi:17) â€” attributed, not asserted as fact. âœ“  \nâ€” First feature shot entirely on IMAX cameras: TOI (claim toi:7). âœ“  \nâ€” IMAX advance sales nearly doubled previous record: LAT (claim bd7c6f:15). âœ“  \nâ€” 70mm roughly 4% of total gross: LAT (claim bd7c6f:16). âœ“  \nâ€” Hoyte van Hoytema IMAX tests August 2024: LAT (claim bd7c6f:11). âœ“  \nâ€” Nolan viewed results two months later: LAT (claim bd7c6f:12). âœ“  \nâ€” Gelfond quote on ecosystem: LAT (claim bd7c6f:13). âœ“  \nâ€” Animatronics, puppetry, Viking boat: LAT (claim bd7c6f:17). âœ“  \nâ€” 95% Rotten Tomatoes: TOI (claim toi:19). âœ“  \nâ€” Summer box office 7%â€“9% behind 2019: LAT (claim bd7c6f:19). âœ“  \nâ€” Moana at $19M in second/third weekend: LAT (claim bd7c6f:20) and TOI (claim toi:20). âœ“  \nâ€” Spider-Man: Brand New Day opening late July: TOI (claim toi:16) and WinterIsComing (claim fb291f6c:20). âœ“\n\nNo invented events, numbers, quotes, or causal mechanisms were identified beyond the $117M inversion. No fairness concerns: the casting controversy paragraph attributes commentary to sources and notes the outcome (strong box office) without editorializing. No unnamed adversarial claims are asserted as fact. The manuscript does not allege wrongdoing and requires no response from a criticized party.\n\n**Form Compliance**\n\nThe citation requirement (THE STORY linked before first factual claim) is met: the LA Times and Variety links appear in the opening sentence. All attributable paragraphs name their sources inline. No standalone hyperlinked titles appear between paragraphs. The \"As [X] notes\" template construction appears twice (within limit). The word count at 710 falls within the 600â€“850 evidence-budget window. The demand-lens term is not reproduced verbatim in the headline or body. The ban on rhetorical questions is observed throughout.\n\n**Summary of Repairs Required**\n\nThree bounded repairs are needed: (1) correct the $117M domestic figure inversion to $124.5M actual / $117M expectation; (2) insert four ## section headings to satisfy the three-to-five structural requirement; (3) merge the paragraph-4 near-duplicate sentence pair. Repair tools are supplied below. repair_editorial_surfaces follows to regenerate the frontmatter surfaces from the corrected body.","repairTools":[{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"Domestically, Universal Pictures reported an opening of approximately $117 million, which the Los Angeles Times described as a record-setting live-action debut for the studio and well above internal estimates.","after":"Domestically, Universal Pictures reported an opening of $124.5 million in the U.S. and Canada â€” surpassing the studio's internal estimate of $117 million, per the Los Angeles Times, which described the result as the year's highest-grossing opening weekend for any live-action or R-rated film."},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"Internationally, individual territory records fell across multiple markets.","after":"## Nolan's Best International Opening\n\nInternationally, individual territory records fell across multiple markets."},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"The scale of the debut stands out given the film's profile.","after":"## R-Rated, Unfranchised, and Expensive\n\nThe scale of the debut stands out given the film's profile."},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"The production budget was reported between $200 million and $250 million, placing it among the most expensive R-rated productions ever made, according to [Times of India](https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/entertainment/english/hollywood/box-office/the-odyssey-storms-box-office-with-a-usd-264-1-million-global-debut-dethrones-the-dark-knight-rises-as-christopher-nolans-highest-film-opening/articleshow/132501487.cms) and